  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Friday)\nIssued at 1152 PM CDT FRI OCT 30 2026\n\nA prominent plume of deep atmospheric moisture, originating from\nthe remnants of Tropical Storm Vance and associated with an\napproaching upper-level shortwave trough, is projected to\ninteract with a rich swath of high (PWAT) values streaming in\nfrom the Gulf of Mexico. This dynamic setup will foster elevated\nprecipitation chances across central Alabama throughout the\nweekend. While the highest probabilities for widespread rain are\nanticipated to manifest overnight, we will continue to maintain\nlow-end chances (20-30%) during the Saturday afternoon hours; so\nif planning outdoor weekend activities or early trick-or-treating\nyou should take this into account. With that being said, several\nhigher-resolution convective-allowing models continue to suggest\na trend toward more isolated to widely scattered coverage. As we\nmove into Sunday, the shortwave feature will gradually lift\nnortheastward across the region, sustaining low-to-moderate rain\nchances through Sunday evening before diminishing.\n\nTransient upper-level ridging building in early next week will\nyield a brief period of dry and relatively benign weather before\nthe arrival of our next storm system toward mid-week. There are\nstill notable structural details and model discrepancies to\nresolve over the coming days, particularly regarding the precise\nsouthern trajectory of the upper-level low and its overall\ntranslation speed across the Southeast. For the time being, we\nwill carry moderate probabilities (40-60%) for showers and\nthunder, given that robust moisture recovery is expected ahead of\nthe advancing cold front.\n\n16",
